  "plot_summary": "The FF are marveling at Reed's new thought-projector device, but Sue excuses herself before someone suggests she try it.&nbsp; Reed then admits he is about to go searching for an engagement ring.\n\nBut little does he know that \"at that very moment,\" the Sub-Mariner is lamenting that \"a prince must have a bride to <i>share</i> his throne!\" and \"If I could have the surface girl I love at my side, nothing else would matter!\"&nbsp; (Uh oh - this timing <i>can't</i> be good!)&nbsp; When he announces his intentions to his last few remaining loyal subjects, they take off - \"He left us no choice!&nbsp; We <i>must</i> desert him!&nbsp; We cannot be loyal to a monarch who would betray his royal heritage!\"&nbsp; So Namor is alone again - forced to follow his heart.\n\nAnd soon, the Sub-Mariner attacks!&nbsp; He quickly dispatches the Thing and Human Torch, then tells Sue why he has come.&nbsp; She warns him that Reed will find out (when he returns) and come after him, to which he replies \"Richards has had <i>years</i> to court you, to make you love him!&nbsp; All I ask is 24 hours, and I shall <i>have </i>them!!\"&nbsp; And he <i>gasses</i> her to sleep.&nbsp; (Not much on romancing a girl, huh?&nbsp; <i>Geez!</i>)&nbsp; \"...she <i>must</i> marry me!&nbsp; She <i>must</i>!&nbsp; Or else I have sacrificed everything in vain!\"\n\nSoon Reed returns with the engagement ring, only to learn from Ben and Johnny what happened.&nbsp; Reed flies off the handle, completely enraged and snaps at them \"Namor is <i>mine!</i>  He's stolen the one thing I love most in the world!&nbsp; ...<i>Nothing</i> will save him now!\"&nbsp; And Reed heads off to his \"radar-sweep-ray\" to find Namor, with Ben concluding that they've \"got to find a way to reach the Sub-Mariner <i>first</i>!\"&nbsp; But when Ben points out they don't have \"any of the hocus-pocus stuff of Reed's to help us find him,\" it gives Johnny an idea of \"the <i>one</i> person who might be able to find Namor...the one man who <i>knows</i> hocus-pocus!\"&nbsp; And he sky-writes a message to \"Dr. Strange\" to contact him.\n\nAnd sure enough, in his Greenwich Village sanctuary, Dr. Strange takes note and sends his \"ectoplasmic form\" to the Baxter Building, where he sees the departure of Reed and then learns from Ben and Johnny what's going on.&nbsp; He agrees to help find the Sub-Mariner and sends his shadowy form into the depths, soon locating \"a magnificent ornate castle.\"\n\nInside, Sue declares to Namor \"Do you think you can make me confess I love you by <i>imprisoning</i> me?\"&nbsp; But Namor explains he's had no chance to date her the way Richards has.&nbsp; \"All I ask is 24 hours...\"&nbsp; Sue is having none of it and indicates that once Reed finds her, \"<i>nothing</i> can save you!\"\n\nDr. Strange has seen all and departs to tell Ben and Johnny, but notices Reed heading inside as shadowy form withdraws.&nbsp; Reed attacks Namor, who declares \"<i>This</i> time there are no others to help you!&nbsp; <i>This</i> time you die!\"&nbsp; And as Reed and Namor battle, they are interrupted by the sudden return of a squad of undersea warriors - \"We realized we could <i>not</i> desert our prince and still be worthy of the name sub-mariners!\" (<i>How convenient</i>!)&nbsp; Reed is now cornered, but he's determined to \"finish you off forever!\"\n\nMeanwhile, Dr. Strange returns to the Baxter Building to inform the Thing and Human Torch and then to join his physical form so that he can \"send you to Prince Namor by the fastest way imaginable!\"&nbsp; And before they have time to blink, the pair are there with Reed, watching him be attacked by Namor's \"deep-sea stooges havin' target practice with our bouncin' buddy!\"&nbsp; And they join in the fray.\n\nNamor is fine with them being there - he can destroy them all at once.&nbsp; But Reed still is enraged - \"I'm going to make him wish he'd never <i>heard</i> of the human race!\"&nbsp; Namor is actually surprised to see Reed so passionate, that perhaps he now finally understands his own feelings of losing his kingdom - everything he holds dear...\"because of you accursed <i>humans</i>!\"&nbsp; But Reed doesn't care - \"So long as you live, mankind will be in danger!!&nbsp; I'll see to it that Namor the First becomes Namor the Last!\"\n\nBen handles the guards while Johnny flies off and frees Sue, as Namor and Reed continue to battle it out.&nbsp; Reed's pliable body is able to withstand Namor's far stronger blows, until Namor finally realizes that \"We live by different codes!&nbsp; They've <i>never</i> understood me!&nbsp; They'll <i>always </i>hound me...unless I stop them now...stop them forever!\"&nbsp; He has his men aim their undersea blast gun at Reed and Ben and offers them the \"privileges of prisoners of war\" if they surrender unconditionally.&nbsp; Of course they refuse, and Namor unleashes the gun, but it does no harm!&nbsp; It's Sue's invisible force field that has protected them.\n\nNamor challenges her to choose, but Sue states for the record that \"Reed is the only man for me...nothing can ever change that!\"&nbsp; But <i>oh my</i> - we've forgotten about Dr. Strange!&nbsp; He's been watching and sees that \"the sea prince has never been so scorned before!&nbsp; His rage is mounting!&nbsp; He is capable of <i>anything</i>!\"&nbsp; He transports the FF to safety, even as Namor gives the command to seize them!\n\nReed is still unsure that Sue actually <i>meant</i> what she said about loving him.&nbsp; But we see Sue's thoughts - \"Oh, Reed, you blind fool!&nbsp; Of <i>course</i> it's you I love!!&nbsp; But how can I ever <i>convince</i> you?&nbsp; You'll always suspect I may have just been trying to protect you...or Namor!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "a right-about-mid-grade read.&nbsp; It continues to amaze that Namor is so arrogant that he believes he can kidnap a woman and take only 24 hours to sway her into falling in love with him and agreeing to marriage!&nbsp; The plot was so hurriedly thrust on the reader that it barreled forward at breakneck speed.&nbsp; It took only one page from Subby's initial appearance in the story before he was storming into the Baxter Building and kidnapping his intended princess!&nbsp; No time for subtlety <i>here</i>!&nbsp; I know there are a limited number of pages, but <i>geez</i>!&nbsp; I think a page or two less of battle action would have been okay.\n\nReed's out-of-control rage was eye-opening.&nbsp; I understand that he was mad with rage due to the audacity of Namor thinking he could steal his girl away from him, but to be prepared to finish him off for good - perhaps his intelligence would have prevented him from striking the fatal blow had it actually come to that, but <i>boy</i> was he ever pissed!\n\nThe guest appearance of Doctor Strange was fine, with the exception of how he was initially summoned.&nbsp; Johnny sent out the request for help with only the hint that a \"Dr. Strange\" even existed.&nbsp; Also, having Strange whisk them away to safety kept this to a one-issue story instead of it extending into the next issue (which would also have been okay - and might have helped with some of the very-rushed characterization).&nbsp; This was a case where there was simply too much plot to cover and too few pages to cram it into.\n\nThe artwork in this issue was sub-par, particularly with the images of the Thing, whose look appeared more rough, less detailed.&nbsp; It might have been the inking of \"Geo. Bell\" (George \"Bell\" Roussos).&nbsp; Most of the characters looked okay in this issue, but Reed and the Thing in particular looked less defined and hurried, and with a scene-stealing presence such as the Thing, that's probably something they should get right.&nbsp; I found it distracting.\n\nFinally, what's with the Sub-Mariner's red swimming trunks on the cover?&nbsp; His trunks have always been green before, and this is the only place in the magazine where they aren't!&nbsp; <i>Oops</i>?\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe book was okay - it rushed way too much, pushed the limits of Reed's personality to near breaking point, all the while allowing enough room for its main guest-star to briefly shine (but also using him to prevent the story from spilling over to another issue, which might be viewed as a bit of a cop-out).&nbsp; But mostly, I think it diminished the character of the Sub-Mariner.&nbsp; Namor went from a near-sympathetic figure to just a downright arrogant jackass.&nbsp; It was perhaps a bit much, and thus the book is capped at mid-grade - too much going against it (including the distracting art) to elevate it above that.\n\nAs for this issue's letters page, Gregory Suriano says \"I have to congratulate you on those witty little 'remarks' you sometimes insert into the boxes with the names of the artists, writer and letterer.\" - true enough - those little things always made <i>me </i>smile as well!&nbsp; Goofy?&nbsp; Sure!&nbsp; But fun as well.\n\nJim Grondin's letter applauds Marvel for its various magazines and ends his letter with \"I also think that you are very conceited!\" - to which the editor replies:&nbsp; <i>Jim, we can't understand why people seem to think that we talented Marvel geniuses are conceited!</i> - heh, you gotta love it!",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}